\title{Rules for levels}
\import{prelude}

\ul{
  \li{Both \code{\kwi{String}} and \code{\kwi{Int}} are sets.}
  \li{Any element \code{X : \kwi{Set}} is a set and any \code{P : \kwi{Prop}} is a proposition.}
  \li{The universes \code{\kwi{Set}} and \code{\kwi{Prop}} are themselves theories.}
  \li{For any \em{set} \code{X} and two elements \code{x} and \code{y}, the equality type \code{x = y} is a proposition.}
  \li{The level of a record type is the largest level of all its fields. For example, the record type:
    \pre{
      \kw{sig}
        name : \kwi{String}
        age : \kwi{Int}
      \kw{end}
    }
    is a \em{set} because both \code{\kwi{String}} and \code{\kwi{Int}} are sets, and the record type
    \pre{
      \kw{sig}
        V : \kwi{Set}
        v0 v1 : V
      \kw{end}
    }
    is a \em{theory}, because \code{\kwi{Set}} is a theory (even though) \code{V} itself is a set. The empty record type is a \em{proposition}.}
  \li{Functions are quite restricted, and are always at least theory level. For any \em{set} \code{X} and dependent \em{theory} \code{Y x}, the function type \code{(x : X) -> Y x} is a \em{theory}. (Recall that any proposition or set can silently be considered a theory, if necessary.) Otherwise, it is a top-level type. As examples:
    \ul{
      \li{\code{\kwi{Int} -> \kwi{Int}} is a theory: its domain and codomain are sets.}
      \li{\code{\kwi{Int} -> \kwi{Set}} is a theory: its domain is a set and its codomain is a theory.}
      \li{\code{\kwi{Set} -> \kwi{Int}} is a top-level type: its domain is a theory.}
      \li{\code{\kwi{Set} -> \kwi{Set}} is a top-level type: its domain is a theory.}
    }
    We have encountered these top-level functions previously using a more pleasant syntax as [parameterised theories](0034).
  }
}
